  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1138 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nFrost/freeze potential into early next week...Frost/freeze still\non track for tonight. Light winds, clear skies with high pressure\ndrifting overhead and PWATs down to 0.30 inch point to min temps\nbottoming out. MOS guidance typically does well on these type of\nnights and shows readings in the mid-upper 20s for sites in Vilas\ntoward IMT. Converted the Frost Advisory to Freeze Warning along \nU.P. border and continued the Frost Advisory elsewhere north of\nhighway 29. Mid-high clouds increase late from the west, which\nmay allow for temps steadying out late tonight.\n\nAfter a reprieve on Saturday night, next chance for another \nwidespread frost/freeze is Sunday night into Monday morning, \nwith temperatures in the mid 30s even sneaking down toward the \nFox Valley. Chance of seeing temps less than 33F are 40-80% Sunday\nnight and again on Monday night. Expect more frost/freeze headlines\nto be issued during this time. \n\nDry pattern ahead...Rain chances over the course of the extended \nwill be sparse, with next chances for light rain arriving \nlate Saturday afternoon into Saturday evening. Transient \nshortwave energy will accompany the passage of a clipper-like low \nover the upper Midwest Saturday evening and night, generating \nisentropically forced showers for areas mainly north of Hwy 29.\nThough low chance, MUCAPEs as high as 500J/kg into northwest WI\nlate Saturday could support a rumble of thunder surviving into \nfar northern WI early Saturday evening. Abundance of low level \ndry air will result in a meager 20-30% chance for receiving 0.25\" \nof QPF through Sunday morning. Otherwise, high pressure will be \nthe main driver of the synoptic pattern through at least the \nmiddle of next week, bringing abnormally dry weather from the \nPlains to the Midwest. This does not bode well for northern \nWisconsin where D2 (Severe) to D4 (Exceptional) drought conditions\nexist. Next chances for more widespread rain don't arrive until \nWednesday, though if this week is anything to go by, high pressure\ncould end up putting up a good fight. Latest deterministic models\nhave backed off on these chances compared to what was shown before.",
